WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.960
Narrator: For this scene in "Coco,"

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.010
Pixar's computer kept
getting Mamá Imelda's skirt

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.470
caught between her skeleton joints.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.640
So Pixar developed a whole
new simulation system

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.680
and digitally wrapped every joint

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.770
with a layer of invisible fabric.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.540
Every single Pixar movie
has pushed the boundaries

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.370
of 3D animation with
detailed work like this,

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.690
from lighting food with two light sources

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.860
to simulating realistic fur.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.080
Let's pick up where we
left off in part one,

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.380
with Merida's realistic-looking
locks in "Brave."

00:00:32.778 --> 00:00:35.010
Merida had a lot of hair.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.410
If you unwound a strand of it,

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.830
it would measure 4 feet in length.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.150
But the simulation tech hadn't yet tackled

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:43.440
the unique physics of curly hair,

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.000
where each curl has a springlike structure

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.540
but the overall movement of
the hair is light and soft

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.230
in a way that seems to defy gravity.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:54.620
Pixar's first step was to
build Merida's hair in layers,

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.560
each with a different tightness of curl

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.170
to get that natural-looking variation.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.850
The team then spent three years

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.790
developing a special simulator called Taz

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:05.560
that'd be powerful enough to handle

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.250
the 1,500 hand-placed
curls on Merida's head.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.140
Taz accounted for all the interactions

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.280
between different strands

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.900
while making sure that
every curl held its shape.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.160
The system also changed

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.430
the effect of gravity on Merida's hair,

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.730
using a gravity coefficient
closer to the one on the moon

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.090
than the one on Earth.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.530
With a lower gravity, the
hair could keep its mass

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.950
but flow and bounce in a natural way.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.500
To give the hair its softness,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.091
the lighting team used a mix

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:33.797
of subsurface scattering
and colored shadows

00:01:33.797 --> 00:01:35.330
to imitate the complex way

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.121
curly hair scatters and transmits light.

00:01:38.121 --> 00:01:41.350
It took two months just to
nail the hair in this scene,

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.020
when Merida pulls back her hood

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.390
to reveal the full volume of curls.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.340
But that work set the stage for Pixar

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.140
to represent all hair textures
on screen in the future.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.030
In "Cars," Pixar used ray
tracing in small doses

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.750
to create reflections and shadows

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.980
in dynamic scenes with
lots of shiny vehicles.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.850
But for the vast majority of shots,

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.040
Pixar's artists still built reflections

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.810
and mapped out shadows by hand.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.370
That changed with "Monsters University."

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:11.886
The lighting team wanted more time

00:02:11.886 --> 00:02:13.419
to focus on the artistic,

00:02:13.419 --> 00:02:16.090
rather than the engineering,
side of lighting,

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:19.270
like creating new "scare lights"
for scary monster scenes.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:20.440
To streamline the process,

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.310
the goal was to ray-trace
pretty much everything:

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:25.320
all the shadows and light refractions,

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.210
even the reflections in a character's eye.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.440
This required an overhaul
of the lighting system

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.280
the studio had used since
the first "Toy Story."

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.530
Pixar built a new system
called Global Illumination

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.320
that modeled the natural
distribution of light in a scene,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.530
all based on real-world physics.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.270
Now, every time an artist
placed a light source in a shot,

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:45.430
the computer would know
how that light would travel

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.013
and interact with other elements,

00:02:47.013 --> 00:02:49.230
and it would simulate the
resulting bright spots,

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.800
shadows, and reflections.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.470
Global Illumination became
the default lighting system

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.970
for every Pixar movie,
making Pixar's light

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.730
more closely resemble the
kind we see in real life.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:02:59.890
But this model didn't account

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.060
for more fantastical scenarios,

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.718
like we'd see in "Inside Out."

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:08.580
Joy, the main character of "Inside Out,"

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.640
is supposed to resemble a star.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.350
She's made up of glowing particles

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.050
that radiate off her skin.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.160
This character had to shed
warm yellow-blue light

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.030
wherever she appeared in a scene,

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.450
and the light had to track
with her bouncy movement,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.950
which wasn't easy, since she's
literally a ball of energy.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.690
So Pixar's RenderMan team
came up with an effect

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.690
they called geometric
area lights, or geolight.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.380
This tool allowed lighting artists

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.750
to select a character model

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.050
and turn it into a mobile light source.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.140
That way, Joy's light
could travel with her.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.660
If she walks up to a prop,
she projects her glow on it,

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.810
and she casts light onto
other characters, too,

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.870
like this pivotal scene with Sadness.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:47.440
Her light was also accurate
to her body structure.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.110
For example, when she picks something up,

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.370
we see the light emanate
through her fingers.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.520
This made the outward
glow look more believable,

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.112
taking Pixar's lighting system
yet another step forward.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:02.460
Nature plays a bigger role
in "The Good Dinosaur"

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.390
than in any other Pixar film,

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.990
so the filmmakers really
needed to nail the landscape

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.440
of the American Northwest.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.880
They were especially focused

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.660
on the huge, constantly changing clouds

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.220
they observed on a research trip.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.080
Traditionally, Pixar movies
used matte-painted clouds,

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:18.690
meaning the artist would paint clouds

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.040
onto background plates.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.410
But "The Good Dinosaur"
took place entirely outside,

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.560
with clouds in almost every scene.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.360
So they decided for the first time

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.960
to create entirely volumetric clouds.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.150
That meant these clouds were 3D objects

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.530
animators could move around,

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.690
like a character or a key set piece.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.530
But unlike a character or set,

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.710
a volumetric object
has no defined surface.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.670
It's just made up of
billions of tiny particles.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.830
The clouds team built a huge library

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.210
of what they called "cloud Lego parts."

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.190
They joined these
volumetric forms together

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.510
to create huge clouds of every type:

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.340
rain clouds, stratus
clouds, and cumulus clouds.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.410
With the clouds actually in the scene,

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.907
viewers can move in
and around the 3D forms

00:04:59.907 --> 00:05:01.162
as the camera moves,

00:05:01.162 --> 00:05:03.936
making this world that
much more immersive.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.120
Hank from "Finding Dory" was
"probably the hardest thing"

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.430
Pixar had ever animated at that point,

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.150
according to Michael Stocker,

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:16.020
the supervising animator on the film.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.810
He definitely wouldn't have
been possible in "Finding Nemo."

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.610
Most four- or two-legged
characters, like humans,

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.070
have joints and defined
limits to their movement,

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.020
but octopi don't have skeletons

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.690
or any clear structure to how they move.

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:30.560
To capture that extreme flexibility,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.630
Pixar had to rethink its whole approach

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.070
to character construction.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:35.590
This started with Hank's rig,

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:37.440
the basic puppet that animators use

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.210
to control the character's movement.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.960
While humans have clearly
defined elbows that bend,

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.535
Hank's elbow had to travel

00:05:43.535 --> 00:05:45.110
down the length of his tentacle,

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.610
so Pixar's engineers
developed a special rig

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:49.580
that used a cylinder as Hank's elbow

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:51.810
and relied on a special curve function

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:54.070
to dictate how that muscle should move.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.860
This let animators stay faithful

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.860
to that key rolling motion
of octopus tentacles.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:01.100
The filmmakers also needed

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.040
the webbing between Hank's tentacles

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.460
to flatten and spread
out with each movement,

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.610
so the character team
created a new control

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:09.330
to set the depth of the webbing.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.760
This helped give Hank the
squishy-squashy motion

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.740
that you see here, and it allowed Pixar

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.473
to give life to its most
complex character to date.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:22.700
The filmmakers behind
the third "Cars" movie

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:26.343
wanted to emphasize the aging
details on hero characters,

00:06:26.343 --> 00:06:28.700
like the older racer Louise Nash.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:31.680
Any car's surface is
composed of multiple layers:

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.560
metal flake paint with a clear coating,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:37.300
car stickers, dirt, grime,
and mud composited on top.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.690
But traditionally, when
Pixar showed an object

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.300
with detailed texture
from a more distant shot,

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.290
all the little details would
merge into a single pixel,

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.000
making the object's
surface look too smooth.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.620
So Pixar developed a technology

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:51.400
called bump roughness mapping,

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.900
which converts what
looks like bumps up close

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.930
into surface roughness
in farther-away shots.

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:57.890
Louise, for example,

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:00.260
keeps the texture of her
clear-coat scratches,

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.070
even in medium and wide shots.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:04.550
And this tech wasn't only
applicable to "Cars."

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:06.700
Pixar could use the bump-roughness method

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.150
to preserve details on
all kinds of 3D objects,

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.300
as we'll see for "Incredibles 2."

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:15.870
Clothing simulation was always going

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:17.810
to be a big undertaking in "Coco."

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:21.000
The movie demanded more cloth
than any other Pixar feature,

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.890
and many scenes would
involve a lot of movement.

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:24.723
On top of this,

00:07:24.723 --> 00:07:27.650
the characters weren't
just humans, but skeletons.

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.350
Bones have all these crevices

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:32.040
where clothes can bunch up
and collide with each other,

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:33.520
creating too many collisions

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:36.160
for Pixar's simulator Fizt to handle.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.340
So the studio spent three years

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.420
creating a new continuous
collision detection program,

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.880
which helped the computer
automatically resolve issues

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.130
like cloth bunching up
around a skeleton's hand

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:47.990
or snagging on a bony knee.

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:50.760
Animators also digitally
wrapped the skeletons' joints

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.900
with super-stretchy invisible material,

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:54.550
so that when the bones separated,

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.550
the pants would touch
that invisible barrier

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.320
and not fall into the gaps.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.030
This was a creative solution
to a unique problem,

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.170
but it laid the groundwork

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:05.886
for even more advanced
cloth simulation later on.

00:08:08.844 --> 00:08:11.470
Fashion was also a big
part of "The Incredibles,"

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:12.443
but in the first movie,

00:08:12.443 --> 00:08:14.710
the characters' suits
weren't actual clothing.

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:17.700
They were patterns shaded
onto the characters' bodies,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.080
which is why, in some shots,

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.060
the fabric behaves more like skin

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:22.920
and the Incredibles logo stretches out

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.640
in unrealistic ways.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:25.610
For the second movie,

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:28.030
the filmmakers wanted their
clothing to work better

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:31.250
with the extreme movements of
characters like Elastigirl.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:33.950
That began with constructing
actual digital garments

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:35.900
that sat on top of the characters' skin,

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.070
like normal clothing.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:39.060
The next key step

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:41.750
was manipulating UV
coordinates on the clothes.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:45.090
In "Incredibles 2," a
tech called 3D UV-scaling

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:46.670
preserved the details of a cloth

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:48.500
when stretched or compressed.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.600
Pixar also found a way to
use bump roughness mapping,

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.200
the technology from "Cars 3,"

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.200
to keep very fine
illuminated details intact,

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.820
even when Elastigirl extended
her body to wild proportions.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.560
That made it possible for Elastigirl

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.580
to get a brand-new suit,
this iridescent unitard

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:06.520
that keeps its subtle shimmery texture

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.850
through all the hyper-flexible movements.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:11.140
Compare that to this shot
of Elastigirl stretching out

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:12.480
in the first "Incredibles."

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:13.840
The surface of her new suit

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:15.970
looks much more like actual fabric.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:18.300
You can see how Pixar
has stretched the limits

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.791
of what its cloth technology can do.

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:27.530
The fourth "Toy Story"
brought up issues of scale,

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.000
thanks to all the scenes
set in the antique store,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.540
and thus all the toys.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:34.290
There were over 10,000
antiques in this store,

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:37.110
and Pixar had to find a way
to program each and every one

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.630
with varying levels of wear and tear.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.310
Animators tackled this challenge

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:43.710
by assigning three different
ages to the antiques:

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:47.200
fairly new, moderately
aged, and extremely aged.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:49.540
The computer than gave
the items surface details

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.780
that fit those ages, from
micro-scratches on china platters

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:54.920
to wear and tear on old rugs.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.640
The ability to add detail
to so many different objects

00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:59.640
is what gave the world of "Toy Story 4"

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.286
its visual complexity and appeal.

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:07.630
Pixar has had its share
of unique characters,

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:11.635
but Dad from "Onward" is made
up of only a pair of legs.

00:10:11.635 --> 00:10:13.790
The rest of his body is just stuffing

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:16.420
with a sweatshirt, vest,
and gloves thrown over it,

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.430
which made it difficult to animate.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:20.480
Dad's legs could be rigged and animated

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:21.830
like a traditional character,

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:24.530
using reference footage filmed
in front of a green screen.

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:27.900
But his upper half had no skin
or structure underneath it,

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:31.720
so it called for simulation,
not animation, in many shots.

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:34.640
The arms, for example,
just had to flop around,

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:37.240
a movement that's more
believable when simulated.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:38.450
So the filmmakers developed

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:41.540
a hybrid simulation-animation rig for Dad.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:42.373
With this rig,

00:10:42.373 --> 00:10:44.630
animators could manipulate
Dad's lower half

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:45.710
like they usually would,

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:47.350
but the simulation team controlled

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:49.640
everything above the belt when needed.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.040
The movement of Dad's stuffing

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:53.073
was based on a volumetric sim,

00:10:53.073 --> 00:10:54.980
a simulation for something with volume,

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:56.600
while the clothes on top of the stuffing

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:59.930
were largely controlled with
standard cloth simulation.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:01.710
Pixar also improved the interaction

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:04.120
between volumetric sims and cloth sims

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:05.970
so that if you pushed on Dad's clothing,

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:07.949
the stuffing would get
squished believably,

00:11:07.949 --> 00:11:10.190
and vice versa, if you
pushed on the stuffing,

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:11.870
the clothing would move out of the way.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:13.810
The hybrid rig improved the relationship

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.120
between separate
simulations, and ultimately,

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:19.520
it showed how simulation and
animation could join forces

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:23.062
to create realistic movement
on an unrealistic character.

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:27.800
Pixar needed to make
its musician characters

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.150
play instruments, most notably Joe,

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.760
who had to go through the
complicated, rapid motions

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.260
of playing the piano.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.280
At first, the team tried to use simulation

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:37.380
to handle the interactions

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:39.160
between Joe's fingers and the keys,

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:40.880
relying on a collision system

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:43.070
like the one used for the cloth in "Coco."

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:44.880
But the collision system didn't work,

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:46.880
because Joe's fingers were too heavy

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:48.610
for the computer's piano keys.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:50.620
So instead of simulating the movement,

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:52.790
character technical director Jason Davies

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:55.560
developed what he called
a finger contact rig.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.550
When one of Joe's fingers
pushed down on a key,

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:59.430
the rig would calculate the correct angle

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:00.610
for the key to rotate,

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:02.280
so it looked like Joe was pushing it.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:03.520
The modeling and rigging teams

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:06.690
also gave Joe's hand 292 controls,

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:08.970
including variables
they'd never used before,

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:11.160
even adding a control for each tendon

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.030
to convey the tension in
Joe's hand as he played.

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:16.960
This level of hand and wrist
control made his performances

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.380
look passionate and convincing on screen,

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.550
with a level of realism
that pushed the boundaries

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:23.608
of human movement in animation.

00:12:26.237 --> 00:12:28.270
"Luca" is a coming-of-age story

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:31.520
about a sea monster who
can transform into a human,

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:34.160
and the filmmakers needed
to show this transformation

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:35.770
in a fluid way.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:37.750
They also wanted to
give animators the power

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:40.290
to dictate all the details of that change,

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:42.440
such as where the transformation begins

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.510
and the speed at which it happens.

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:46.870
Before, adjustments to
those kinds of details

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:48.540
would have to go through animation,

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:50.040
then go to the effects team,

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.300
and only then would animators get to see

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.290
how it all looked after rendering.

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:57.230
But for "Luca," they created
a transformation rig,

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:58.970
so animators could fine-tune

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:01.440
the different aspects
of the change one by one

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.805
and see the result right
there in front of them.

00:13:03.805 --> 00:13:06.386
That way, they could
perfectly time the transition

00:13:06.386 --> 00:13:08.930
from the layers of
changes in the human skin

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:11.250
to the flipping scales and octopus spots

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:12.660
in the sea-monster skin.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:13.710
This was a new way

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:15.940
to streamline the whole production process

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:19.890
and put more artistic control
in the hands of animators.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:23.160
After all, Pixar views
technology first and foremost

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:24.980
as a storytelling tool.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:26.410
Whether creating a new kind of light

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:28.410
to better convey a character's emotions

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:29.880
or rethinking cloud models

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:32.420
to give audiences a
stronger sense of place,

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:33.950
Pixar's technical innovations

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.580
have always served its stories first,

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.370
and that's the key to the studio's record

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.010
of creating both
groundbreaking innovations

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.886
and beloved movies.

